 12-point Democratic partisan lean in an open-seat race", "Consistent polling showing Becerra ahead by roughly 20 points, with recent surveys showing the lead widening", "All six expert ratings place the race in the safest Democratic category", "Prediction markets price Becerra at 95-96 percent"], "rationale": "Every recent live-caller poll of likely voters puts Becerra up by 18 to 22 points, and the most recent surveys from September show the gap widening rather than narrowing. With a deep-blue fundamentals baseline, unanimous Safe/Solid D ratings, and markets near 96 percent, the evidence points firmly to a Safe Democratic hold despite Hilton's energetic outsider campaign.", "watch": "A major external shock or a sustained affordability-driven shift toward Hilton could tighten the race, but he would need a historic polling miss to close a 20-point gap.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's race pits Democrat Xavier Becerra against Republican Steve Hilton after Gavin Newsom's term-limited exit. Polling has been consistent and recent: SurveyUSA in late September showed Becerra up 22, PPIC up 22, and Berkeley IGS up 18 in August, with September headlines describing a widening lead. The only outlier is a Republican-sponsored August poll showing a narrower eight-point race. Fundraising is roughly comparable, with Hilton slightly ahead in dollars raised and cash on hand, but money has not translated into a polling shift.\n\nAll six expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Becerra's chances at 95 to 96 percent. Hilton has run a high-profile outsider campaign focused on cost of living and overhauling state institutions, while Becerra has kept a lower profile. In a state with a 12-point Democratic lean, that message has so far gained little traction, and the race remains a safe Democratic hold with about seven weeks to go.", "history": [{"da